Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-07-2001||07:54 AM||||When you buy movies.. do you get widescreen or regular?<P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||12-07-2001||08:51 AM||||I chose regular viewing (fills the screen) if at all possible.  Widescreen is cool if you have a huge tv or you're at the theater, but for anything under 30" your vision is just too little.  I would rather see less up close than more but smaller.<P>I only have a nickel, keep the change <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Springtyme||12-07-2001||08:55 AM||||Definately regular... <P>------------------<BR>For those of you who don't know, yes I'm crazy.<BR>~~~~~<BR>1988 Coupe 4 cyl.<BR>0-infinity in 2 seconds<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/springtyme3.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||firstfiero||12-07-2001||09:02 AM||||    I prefer regular but it seems I have a hard time finding anything but wide screen on dvd. I have 70 incher and to be honest I want to use the whole damn tv...not just the middle.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||12-07-2001||09:05 AM||||Definitely widescreen... seeing movies as God intended them to be seen.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>(Definitely took me a while to decide that, though... it wasn't until I saw 'Highlander' in the movie theatre (a re-release a few years ago) - a movie that, until then, I had only seen on VHS - and I noticed just how much stuff had been hacked off the sides of the screen to make it fit the fullscreen version that I decided I could deal with the stupid black bars if it allowed me to see everything I was supposed to be seeing... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>Now I don't buy a movie *unless* I can get it in Widescreen...||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-07-2001||09:24 AM||||I also posted this topic on another forum I am on.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.vcdhelp.com" TARGET=_blank>www.vcdhelp.com</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.dvdrhelp.com/phpBB/viewtopic.php?topic=68822&forum=6" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dvdrhelp.com/phpBB/viewtopic.php?topic=68822&forum=6</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||12-07-2001||09:55 AM||||Widescreen all the way!!!  Ever since DVD caught on, I can't stand to watch a full-screen VHS movie.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Oh, and it really annoys me when people get a DVD and use the zoom feature to make it fill the whole screen.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <P>I'm picky about my video viewing preferances, can you tell?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||Formula88||12-07-2001||10:20 AM||||Definately Widescreen!  When you realize how much of the picture you're missing, it's just not worth it to watch Pan & Scan.  It's not so bad on Flat movies, because they are close to the size of a TV screen and not much cropping is necessary, but on a CinemaScope film, it's amazing.  I even prefer WideScreen on my 20" TV, so it's not about having a big TV.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000008||BN Boomer||12-07-2001||10:36 AM||||I can tolerate full frame/pan&scan, but I definitely prefer widescreen/original aspect ratio, too. It's odd how people who say they want to "use their whole screen" don't realize how much they are missing because how much of the edges are lopped off of the original movie. Probably the only yime I wouldn't prefer widescreen is on the 9" TV in my Suburban. That picture is so itty bitty.<P>But I agree with LZeitgeist, now I won't buy a movie on DVD unless I can get it in widescreen/OAR. Happy Gilmore is one that comes to mind that I want to buy it on DVD but won't because I've only seen it available in full frame.||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||12-07-2001||10:57 AM||||I prefer widescreen myself. Probably my favorite screen size is the widescreen format used by Pixar's animated movies. It is a special version of widescreen that takes up about 3/4 of the screen, and looks really good on my tube.||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||12-07-2001||11:19 AM||||<B>Widescreen</B> all the way!<P>I've got a 36" Sony Trinitron Flat TV (just finished paying it off last month!) and they are just beautiful too.<P>Home Theater... one of my few other non-fiero hobbies.  I've got the above mentioned TV, a Sony DTS ES SurroundSound reciever,  ProScan DVD (soon to be replaced by a Sony Progressive Scan DVD), a ProScan VCR, a Panasonic LD player (yeah, the big ones), and currently a set of KLH surround sound speakers(5 channels w/10" sub).  I'm in the process of remodeling my basement specifically for a small dedicated home theater.  And the speakers will be replaced with a set of new <A HREF="http://www.jbl.com/home/products/series.asp?SerId=STU" TARGET=_blank>JBL S-Series Speakers</A><P>There is nothing like submersing ones-self in quality surround-sound! <P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||12-07-2001||11:32 AM||||I prefer full screen. I have 35" and if I get a DVD in wide screen, I zoom it in till it fills the top and bottom.||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||12-07-2001||01:31 PM||||Widescreen imagry (about 2:1 infact, super 35 is well suited at 2.35:1) is best for the layout of both the human eyeball and the visual cortex.  It's just about right such that our eyes are nearly filled, yet don't have to wander around seeking out imagry (fatiuging, that's why sitting too close causes eye strain.)<P>I do some Home and Venue Theatre design, my personal system is a 120" Ampro front projector.  Looks best with 2k playback from the SGIs, but progressive scan dvd is pretty cool too.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/stuff/images/theatre.jpg"> <P><BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">     <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||3
